\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Exact path laws and autonomous dynamics}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The proof of \ref{fdip-00HI} after #{J^*J=I} uses only an isometric embedding
and a self-adjoint semigroup. It therefore applies to a gauge theory once its
[physical Hilbert space](fdip-0083), invariant state and a retained embedding
compatible with the Gauss constraint have been specified. The gauge projection in
\ref{fdip-0083} supplies the relevant kind of constraint; a scalar configuration
projection alone supplies no such gauge data.}

\p{The effective interaction #{F_D} and precision #{Q_{D,X}} in \ref{fdip-00HP} are not required to be nearest-neighbor in retained time.
The generated higher interactions have the exact-integral and finite-order meanings
already established for the interacting fiber law in \ref{fdip-00GN}; the Gaussian
covariance calculation in \ref{fdip-00HK} shows temporal memory without an
interaction weight. Exponentiating a truncated interaction requires its own global
stability argument, as in \ref{fdip-00GQ}. For a general continuous coercive
potential, \ref{fdip-00HM} and \ref{fdip-00HI} remain available; the displayed
Gaussian-reference effective-action formula is asserted only when the declared
positive-weight Gaussian factorization is supplied. In particular no
infinite-dimensional Lebesgue density on continuous path space is being assumed.}
